1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:07,451
High in the Andes of Peru, the Apurímac river runs through a deep gorge.

2
00:00:09,151 --> 00:00:14,521
Across it hangs a bridge made of grass, twisted by hand into cord: the keshwa chaca.

3
00:00:16,021 --> 00:00:20,587
By their own account, the people there have crossed on rope since the time of the Incas.

4
00:00:21,887 --> 00:00:25,088
It was once the only way between the communities around it.

5
00:00:26,588 --> 00:00:30,439
Grass wears out, so the bridge has to be made new, by many hands.

6
00:00:32,139 --> 00:00:36,846
In 1968, a steel bridge is built, almost a hundred metres away.

7
00:00:38,546 --> 00:00:42,680
Now cars can cross the gorge with their loads, and few people need the rope.

8
00:00:44,180 --> 00:00:50,751
A geographer who visits in 1970 finds it decaying: not raised, he writes, for a few years.

9
00:00:52,451 --> 00:00:55,812
He expects the skill of making it to die with that generation.

10
00:00:57,912 --> 00:01:04,373
In October 1972, one of the bridge's keepers tells two visitors what his people have decided.

11
00:01:05,673 --> 00:01:10,668
"My people feel sad about abandoning the keshwa chaca for a new steel bridge upstream."

12
00:01:12,168 --> 00:01:14,723
"So we're going to rebuild it when the New Year comes."

13
00:01:16,823 --> 00:01:23,435
When the new year came, they did. Asked years later, the people there name nobody from outside who pushed them to.

14
00:01:25,135 --> 00:01:29,582
Before, one elder says, few came to help. "Only now does everyone come."

15
00:01:31,482 --> 00:01:37,953
Today, four communities make it new each June: about a thousand people, each family bringing its own cord.

16
00:01:39,653 --> 00:01:43,680
They do it as minka: unpaid work, shared for everyone's benefit.

17
00:01:45,180 --> 00:01:51,045
The communities, UNESCO says, see it as strengthening their ties, and not simply as a way across.

18
00:01:52,545 --> 00:01:57,904
One of the builders says it more plainly: "If we stop preserving it, it would be like if we died."

19
00:02:00,204 --> 00:02:03,919
A steel bridge could carry them across. It couldn't bring them together.

20
00:02:06,019 --> 00:02:11,416
A data team can be read both ways too: as roles on a chart, or as the people in them.

21
00:02:12,916 --> 00:02:18,888
Plan from the chart alone, and you plan for boxes. Know the people first, and the plan can fit them.

22
00:02:26,988 --> 00:02:32,251
Monday 1 February 2027, a year from now, at the university.

23
00:02:33,751 --> 00:02:38,408
Helena Novak starts as its data manager. Two sheets wait on her desk.

24
00:02:40,108 --> 00:02:46,231
A chart of her team: seven boxes, with a role in each. And a list of forty-six open requests.

25
00:02:48,131 --> 00:02:51,557
From those two sheets, she could plan everyone's work by lunch.

26
00:02:53,257 --> 00:02:59,043
She does sort the list that day, as Every area, one job shows. But she changes nobody's work.

27
00:03:00,743 --> 00:03:05,027
A chart shows who reports to whom. It doesn't show who is in the boxes.

28
00:03:07,127 --> 00:03:15,504
There's even a standard for putting people first, ISO 27500: value your people, and count their differences as a strength.

29
00:03:17,404 --> 00:03:21,398
So before she plans anyone's work, she sits down with each of the seven.

30
00:03:24,098 --> 00:03:28,491
One at a time, from Monday to Thursday, where each of them works, for about an hour.

31
00:03:30,391 --> 00:03:33,731
She asks each the same four questions, about them at work.

32
00:03:35,231 --> 00:03:37,696
What are you proud of here? What would you like more of?

33
00:03:39,196 --> 00:03:42,464
What gets in your way? And what should I know, to work well with you?

34
00:03:44,164 --> 00:03:48,466
She never asks about health, family, or anything a person doesn't offer.

35
00:03:50,766 --> 00:03:58,145
Noor Rahman, the data architect, is proud of her map of the university's data. She'd like more people to argue with it.

36
00:04:00,445 --> 00:04:07,915
Sam Okafor runs the data platform. He's proud of each morning the data is simply there, and nobody has to ask.

37
00:04:10,215 --> 00:04:16,386
Jun Park, the analytics engineer, is proudest when someone stops asking him, because now they know.

38
00:04:18,686 --> 00:04:24,888
Yusuf Demir leads data governance. Ask me at the start, he says, and I can say yes more often.

39
00:04:27,188 --> 00:04:32,344
Marta Silva, the analyst, would like to hear what became of a number after she sends it.

40
00:04:34,644 --> 00:04:40,819
Erin Walsh, the platform engineer, started the same week as Marta. They still have lunch on Thursdays.

41
00:04:43,119 --> 00:04:49,030
Tomás Ortega, the report developer, can tell you who reads each report, and what they do with it.

42
00:04:51,330 --> 00:04:52,828
None of it is on the chart.

43
00:04:57,128 --> 00:05:00,257
In those hours, Helena hears one name again and again.

44
00:05:01,757 --> 00:05:05,719
Stuck on a number, Marta asks Jun. So does Erin, about a model.

45
00:05:07,419 --> 00:05:12,170
Noor shows him her map before anyone else. Sam shows him a load that looks odd.

46
00:05:14,070 --> 00:05:18,996
On the chart, Jun is one box of seven. Its one line goes up, to Helena.

47
00:05:20,896 --> 00:05:24,677
A chart shows who manages. It doesn't show who people turn to.

48
00:05:26,977 --> 00:05:32,667
Ubuntu, the idea behind The team comes first, says a person is a person through other people.

49
00:05:34,567 --> 00:05:40,257
Researchers call it shared leadership: people in a team leading one another, whoever holds the title.

50
00:05:41,957 --> 00:05:47,996
Across forty-two samples, teams that share the leading do better, most of all in how they work together.

51
00:05:49,896 --> 00:05:53,499
Leading doesn't need a title. Anyone can lead from where they are.

52
00:05:55,599 --> 00:06:02,997
Helena doesn't move Jun's box. She asks if he wants this, and makes room: in March, he'll be the one beside a newcomer.

53
00:06:05,697 --> 00:06:10,451
On Friday 5 February, Helena brings all seven together in the team's room.

54
00:06:12,151 --> 00:06:16,386
A new manager is a change, and people fear the changes they can't see.

55
00:06:18,086 --> 00:06:22,141
So she says what she can promise: who does what stays as it is this month.

56
00:06:24,241 --> 00:06:28,852
She doesn't promise that nothing will change: only what stays, and for how long.

57
00:06:30,752 --> 00:06:35,920
At the gorge, four years after the steel bridge came, a keeper said what his people would keep.

58
00:06:38,020 --> 00:06:39,937
Helena says it in her first week.

59
00:06:41,637 --> 00:06:47,330
She has planned nobody's work yet. But she knows seven people, and they know what to expect from her.

60
00:06:54,830 --> 00:07:00,804
At the gorge, steel could do the carrying. What people went back for was each other, and the work they share.

61
00:07:02,904 --> 00:07:07,100
A chart and a list can carry a team's work. They can't tell you who is here.

62
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:13,033
On Monday, before you plan anyone's work, sit down with each person.

63
00:07:14,533 --> 00:07:18,313
Ask about them at work: what they're proud of, and what gets in their way.

64
00:07:19,813 --> 00:07:24,077
Notice who people turn to, whatever the chart says, and make room for it.

65
00:07:25,577 --> 00:07:27,536
And say early what won't change yet.

66
00:07:29,436 --> 00:07:34,113
Wellbeing is in the work, the next film, asks the next question: what do they need?

67
00:07:35,813 --> 00:07:37,785
Know the people before you plan the work.
